+### 5. `PanelOrnate` — Panel / Ornate (`40:137`), 9-slice
+The Note says it stretches from 240 x 160 to 960 x 640 and the corner flourishes must not stretch.
+A 9-slice image does exactly that in one ImageLabel. The fill and 4 px border are flat colour, so
+they stretch cleanly along the edges and centre.
+
+- Figma prep: duplicate the frame, **hide the Note (`40:136`)**, set Corner 1–4 constraints to their
+  own corners (Left+Top, Right+Top, Left+Bottom, Right+Bottom), and resize the frame to
+  **112 x 112** (48 corner + 16 stretch + 48 corner).
+- Export at **2x → 224 x 224**. Standalone image (sliced images are kept off sheets).
+- ImageLabel:
+  - `Image = PanelOrnate`
+  - `ScaleType = Enum.ScaleType.Slice`
+  - `SliceCenter = Rect.new(96, 96, 128, 128)` (image px: the 48 px corners at 2x)
+  - `SliceScale = 0.5` (draws the 2x corners at their 48 px authored size; `UIScale` then scales
+    them up on bigger screens)
+  - `Size` = dialog size in authored offsets, between `fromOffset(240, 160)` and
+    `fromOffset(960, 640)`. Both minimums are bigger than two corners (96), so corners never overlap.
+    A `UISizeConstraint` with `MinSize = (240, 160)`, `MaxSize = (960, 640)` enforces the range.
+
+### 6. `SlotEmpty` — Slot / Empty (`40:139`)
+Roblox `UIStroke` has no dashes, so the dashed rounded outline has to be an image. The slot is
+always square (a player setting from 80 to 128), so scale the whole image evenly instead of
+9-slicing it. That keeps the dash pattern even. 9-slicing would stretch the dashes on the edges.
+
+- Figma prep: **hide the Note (`40:138`)**, set the stroke colour to white at **100%** opacity.
+- Export the 96 x 96 frame with Figma's size setting **`256w` → 256 x 256**. That is the largest
+  slot (128) at 2x, so it is never drawn above native resolution. Using `256w` scales the stroke and
+  dashes with the frame, so a 96 slot looks exactly like the design.
+- Standalone image.
+- ImageLabel: `Image = SlotEmpty`, `Size = fromOffset(slotSize, slotSize)` (80–128),
+  `ImageColor3 = 255,255,255`, `ImageTransparency = 0.7` (the design's 30% white).
+
+**Designer sign-off needed:** the stroke scales with the slot: 1.67 px at 80, 2 px at 96, 2.67 px
+at 128. If it must stay 2 px at every size, we would need one export per size setting instead.
+
+### 7. `DotGridTile` — Background / Dot Grid (`40:142`), tiled
+The frame on the page is just a sample. The pattern is a 2 x 2 white dot every 24 px (first dot at
+11, 11, so each 24 px cell has its dot centred), shown at 5% opacity. It repeats at any screen size,
+so we export one tile and let Roblox tile it.
+
+- Figma prep: new 24 x 24 frame, no fill, no clipping effects, with one 2 x 2 white ellipse at
+  (11, 11), at **100% opacity** (the 5% goes in Roblox).
+- Export at **2x → 48 x 48**. Standalone (tiling does not work with a sheet sub-rect).
+- ImageLabel:
+  - `Image = DotGridTile`
+  - `Size = UDim2.fromScale(1, 1)` (fills the menu backdrop)
+  - `ScaleType = Enum.ScaleType.Tile`
+  - `TileSize = UDim2.fromOffset(24, 24)` (authored px; `UIScale` keeps the 24 px rhythm relative
+    to the rest of the UI)
+  - `ImageColor3 = 255,255,255`, `ImageTransparency = 0.95`
+
